(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-2-pdm-exam-2-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A healthy 34-year-old asks for every heart blood test at her annual physical. Which test should not be ordered routinely?","choices":["A natriuretic peptide","A lipid profile","A blood pressure check","A glucose level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Routine natriuretic peptides in healthy patients are something not to order; they are for suspected heart failure and dyspnea triage. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How much time does one small box on electrocardiogram paper represent?","choices":["0.2 seconds","0.1 seconds","1 second","0.04 seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and five of them make a large box at 0.2 seconds, so five large boxes cover one second. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which structures can be at least partly seen on a chest radiograph for cardiac purposes?","choices":["Cardiac silhouette, great vessels, lungs and pleura","The coronary arteries and the cardiac valves","The cardiac conduction system","The pericardial fluid volume"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, along with the pulmonary vasculature. Note what is absent: the chambers, the valves and the coronary arteries are not among them. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Besides diagnosing coronary artery disease, which use is described for radionuclide myocardial perfusion imaging?","choices":["Measuring pulmonary artery occlusion pressure","Identifying cardiac tumors such as myxoma","Locating the origin of an arrhythmia","Predicting future cardiac events"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is a stated indication, since the study identifies patients at increased risk of myocardial infarction and those who may need angiography or surgery. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A pharmacological stress test is planned using a drug that increases the workload of the heart rather than dilating the coronary arteries. Which drug is it?","choices":["Adenosine","Dipyridamole","Dobutamine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It raises the work the heart performs, which is how it provokes ischemia, whereas adenosine and dipyridamole act by dilating the coronary arteries. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the QT interval span?","choices":["From the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex","From the start of the QRS complex to the end of the T wave","From the end of the T wave to the next P wave","Between two consecutive R waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which makes it all of the ventricular activity in one cardiac cycle, depolarization and repolarization together. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 69-year-old arrives 14 hours after chest pain began, near the troponin peak. Why may two serial values barely differ?","choices":["Troponin clears within a few hours","Values near the peak may not change","Troponin never rises in late presenters","The assay is invalid after 12 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With late presentation, values are above the 99th percentile and may sit near peak, so the delta between draws can be small. Early presenters show values rising from below the limit. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 3-year-old's father had a myocardial infarction at 38 and has familial hypercholesterolemia. When may the child be screened?","choices":["At age 9 to 11","At age 19","Now, from age 2","Only if symptoms appear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening at age 2 or older is recommended with a family history of premature atherosclerotic disease, severe hypercholesterolemia or familial hypercholesterolemia. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What does a transesophageal study require that a transthoracic one does not?","choices":["Throat anesthesia, sedation and monitoring","Iodinated contrast given intravenously","Electrocardiographic gating of the images","A period of fasting from fluids only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a procedure rather than a scan, which is why it carries a contraindication list and the transthoracic study does not. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has a prolonged thrombin time. Which pair of causes prolongs it?","choices":["Heparin and dabigatran","Warfarin and vitamin K deficiency","Hemophilia A and B","Factor VII and factor XII deficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heparin and dabigatran prolong the thrombin time along with the activated partial thromboplastin time. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has an isolated prolonged partial thromboplastin time with low fibrinogen activity and a normal fibrinogen antigen. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Dysfibrinogenemia","Hypofibrinogenemia","Lupus anticoagulant","von Willebrand disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low fibrinogen activity with normal antigen means the fibrinogen is present but does not work, which is dysfibrinogenemia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which six electrocardiographic leads do the four limb electrodes produce?","choices":["V1 through V6","Leads I, II and III only","Leads I, II and III together with aVR, aVL and aVF","aVR, aVL and aVF only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These six are the hexaxial leads, and they are what the hexaxial reference system uses to determine the axis. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What distinguishes a SEGMENT from an INTERVAL?","choices":["A segment includes a wave; an interval is the flat line between waves","They are two names for the same thing","A segment is measured in millivolts and an interval in seconds","A segment is the flat line between two waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An interval is the larger unit, which is why the PR interval contains the PR segment rather than being the same thing. An interval includes at least one wave plus at least one segment. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a prolonged prothrombin time, with a normal partial thromboplastin time, fibrinogen, D-dimer and platelet count. Which cause fits?","choices":["A vitamin K antagonist","Hemophilia A","Acute thrombosis","Immune thrombocytopenia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An isolated prolonged prothrombin time with everything else normal fits liver disease, a vitamin K antagonist, factor VII deficiency or an oral factor Xa inhibitor. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman needs a fibrinogen level measured. Which method is preferred?","choices":["A prothrombin time derived value","Bleeding time","Mean platelet volume","The Clauss assay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Clauss assay is preferred over a prothrombin time derived fibrinogen. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man is evaluated for a suspected coagulation disorder. Which test is NOT among the initial screening tests?","choices":["Fibrinogen level","Activated partial thromboplastin time","Prothrombin time","D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The initial screens are the platelet count, size and shape, bleeding time, partial thromboplastin time, prothrombin time and fibrinogen level; D-dimer belongs to the thrombotic workup. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"An asymptomatic 52-year-old has a family history of premature coronary artery disease. What kind of lipid profile should be obtained?","choices":["A nonfasting profile only","No profile until age 65","Only a lipoprotein(a) level","A fasting profile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nonfasting is acceptable for most people, but a fasting profile is obtained with triglycerides of 400 mg/dL or above, a known or suspected triglyceride disorder, or a family history of premature atherosclerotic disease or genetic dyslipidemia. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 66-year-old with suspected myocardial infarction has a raised CK-MB (creatine kinase muscle-brain isoenzyme). Why is that result of little diagnostic use today?","choices":["Its cardiac specificity is low","It rises only after a week","It measures wall stretch only","It is falsely low in obesity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Creatine kinase is a cytosolic enzyme of skeletal and cardiac muscle, and even the MB fraction adds no diagnostic value for acute infarction once troponin is available. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does electrophysiological testing identify a reproducible arrhythmia?","choices":["Injection of contrast into each coronary artery","Electrical stimulation of different heart areas","Comparison of tracer uptake at stress and rest","Timing blood pressure during treadmill stages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The rhythm is recorded while different areas are stimulated with electrical impulses, revealing reproducible arrhythmias and their exact origin. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why does calcification degrade a vascular ultrasound image?","choices":["It absorbs the ionizing radiation before it reaches the vessel","It produces a blooming artifact that exaggerates the lesion","It causes acoustic shadowing","It prevents the vein from collapsing under compression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sound does not pass through, so everything behind the calcium is unassessable rather than merely indistinct. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which internodal tract does the majority of the population use?","choices":["The middle tract","The superior anterior, or fast, tract","The inferior posterior, or slow, tract","All three equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The other two usually terminate before reaching the atrioventricular node, so the fast tract does most of the work. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 63-year-old arrives with chest pain and a high-sensitivity troponin is drawn at presentation. When is the repeat drawn?","choices":["At 12 to 24 hours","At 1 to 2 hours","At 4 weeks","Never, one value is enough"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serial testing at hour 0 and 1 to 2 hours is possible because the assay detects lower concentrations, which is what gives the faster rule-in or rule-out. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which body points form Einthoven's triangle, the imaginary triangle created by leads I, II and III?","choices":["Both shoulders and the right lower extremity","Both hips and the left shoulder","Both shoulders and the center of the chest","Both shoulders and the left lower extremity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each side of the triangle compares two of these points, which is why these three leads are bipolar. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"In a regular rhythm, one R wave sits on a heavy line and the next R wave lands on the fourth heavy line after it. What is the heart rate?","choices":["75 beats per minute","100 beats per minute","60 beats per minute","150 beats per minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Counting down the heavy lines from the first R wave gives 300, 150, 100 and then 75 for the fourth line. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Why is lead II used for rhythm interpretation?","choices":["It has a near-perfect view along the heart's vector","It is the only lead that records atrial activity","It is unaffected by patient movement","It is the only bipolar lead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The conduction pathway runs left and down, and lead II looks along that line, so the complexes are at their clearest. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has a prothrombin time ordered. Which pathways does it assess?","choices":["The intrinsic and common pathways","Platelet adhesion and aggregation","Breakdown of fibrin by plasmin","The extrinsic and common pathways"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prothrombin time measures fibrin clot formation after tissue factor is added, so it reflects the extrinsic pathway plus the common pathway factors. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"When is an exercise stress test terminated?","choices":["Only when the patient asks to stop","At maximum performance","After a fixed twelve minutes regardless of response","When blood pressure returns to baseline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three separate stopping conditions, only one of which is the patient simply having had enough. On signs or symptoms of ischemia, or at a predetermined endpoint. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which leads are bipolar?","choices":["aVR, aVL and aVF","V1 through V6","Leads I, II and III","All twelve leads"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They form the imaginary triangle across the shoulders and the left lower body, and everything else on a twelve-lead tracing is unipolar. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does a cardiac vector represent?","choices":["The direction of blood flow through the chambers","The strength of contraction alone","The position of the heart within the chest","The direction and strength of the electrical impulse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both quantities at once, which is why it is drawn as an arrow rather than described as a line. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 59-year-old on high-intensity therapy has triglycerides of 190 mg/dL (desirable below 150 mg/dL) and a low LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol). Which LDL-C estimate is preferred?","choices":["The Friedewald equation alone","Total cholesterol minus triglycerides","Martin/Hopkins or Sampson/NIH equation","HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) doubled"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are preferred for greater accuracy, especially when triglycerides are 150 mg/dL or above or LDL-C is low, which is this patient. NIH is the National Institutes of Health. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a positive fibrin monomer test. What activity does a positive result indicate?","choices":["Platelet aggregation","Vitamin K deficiency","Thrombin activity","Complete absence of fibrinogen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A positive fibrin monomer test is indicative of thrombin activity and is consistent with intravascular coagulation; severe liver disease and inflammatory disorders can also give a positive result. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's troponin report says his value exceeds the upper reference limit. What does that limit represent?","choices":["A value higher than 99% of all cardiac patients","A value higher than 99% of a healthy population","The level at which every patient has an infarction","The value that proves the troponin is from skeletal muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A value above it indicates some level of cardiac muscle damage and signals the need for further diagnostic testing or intervention. Sex-specific limits are generally agreed on. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A PR interval measures seven small boxes. How should that be read?","choices":["Prolonged, since the normal range is three to five small boxes","Normal","Shortened","Uninterpretable without the heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The normal interval runs 0.12 to 0.2 seconds, so seven small boxes is beyond it. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which patient with syncope is an indication for electrophysiological testing?","choices":["One with ischemic or other structural heart disease","One with a normal heart and a clear vasovagal trigger","One with suspected aortic dissection","One with deep venous thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside sick sinus syndrome, survivors of sudden cardiac arrest without an established cause, ectopic beats and a suspected aberrant pathway. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with no bleeding history is scheduled for a procedure, and his prothrombin time is 14 seconds (reference 11 to 13). What can be said of this mild prolongation?","choices":["It predicts major bleeding","It excludes any coagulopathy","It does not predict bleeding","It confirms vitamin K deficiency"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mild or moderate prothrombin time or partial thromboplastin time prolongation does not predict bleeding in a nonbleeding patient. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 51-year-old has a high HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol). What is HDL's role in contrast to the apoB particles?","choices":["Delivering dietary triglyceride","Reverse cholesterol transport","Entering and seeding the artery wall","Carrying apo(a) to the endothelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. HDL performs reverse cholesterol transport, carrying cholesterol on apoA-I (apolipoprotein A-I), a protective role that contrasts with the atherogenic apoB (apolipoprotein B) particles. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has an enlarged spleen and a low platelet count. Which mechanism lowers the circulating count?","choices":["Consumption in clots","Impaired marrow production","Sequestration in the spleen","Immune destruction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypersplenism pools platelets in an enlarged spleen, so fewer circulate. Increased platelet sequestration is a cause of thrombocytopenia. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens to the membrane during depolarization?","choices":["It becomes more negative","It holds steady at its resting voltage","It becomes less negative","It loses all charge separation entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The cell moves away from its resting negativity, which is what the word depolarization describes. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman bleeds easily, and her prothrombin time and international normalized ratio are normal. What does this mean?","choices":["A coagulopathy is excluded","Platelet function is normal","Fibrinolysis is normal","A coagulopathy is not excluded"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal prothrombin time and international normalized ratio do not rule out coagulopathy; platelet disorders and other defects can coexist. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 46-year-old has total cholesterol 200 mg/dL, HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 50 mg/dL and triglycerides 100 mg/dL, well below the 400 mg/dL limit for the Friedewald equation. What is the Friedewald LDL-C (low-density lipoprotein cholesterol)?","choices":["130 mg/dL","150 mg/dL","50 mg/dL","170 mg/dL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Friedewald: total cholesterol minus HDL-C minus triglycerides divided by 5, so 200 minus 50 minus 20 is 130 mg/dL. It is an estimate, not a measurement. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How is coronary angiography performed?","choices":["Under local anesthesia with intravenous sedation","Under general anesthesia with the patient intubated","Without contrast, using ultrasound guidance","Through the esophagus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and pressures are measured as the catheter goes, so it yields hemodynamic information alongside the pictures. With catheters guided by fluoroscopy and contrast injected into each coronary artery. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the fundamental limitation of cardiac computed tomography compared with echocardiography and magnetic resonance?","choices":["It cannot show the coronary arteries","It cannot be used in patients with a normal heart rate","It requires an endoscope","It assesses anatomy but not function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It shows the structure in great detail and cannot tell you how well it is working, which decides when it is the wrong study. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 67-year-old on hemodialysis with no chest pain has two troponin values just above the 99th percentile limit, unchanged between draws. What does this pattern represent?","choices":["Chronic myocardial injury","Acute type 1 infarction","A falsely negative result","Acute heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Troponin can be chronically elevated in chronic kidney disease, heart failure and structural heart disease. Without ischemia and a rising or falling pattern, this is not infarction. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 50-year-old's panel shows total cholesterol 210 mg/dL and HDL-C (high-density lipoprotein cholesterol) 50 mg/dL. What is the non-HDL-C, the value followed against a risk-based goal?","choices":["260 mg/dL","110 mg/dL","160 mg/dL","210 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-HDL-C is total cholesterol minus HDL-C: 210 minus 50 is 160 mg/dL. It needs no triglyceride value and costs nothing extra. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT among the listed indications for vascular ultrasound?","choices":["Deep vein thrombosis study and vascular access","Carotid evaluation after stroke, transient ischemic attack or myocardial infarction","Abdominal aortic aneurysm evaluation and varicose veins","Assessment of coronary artery stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct -- this is not one. The coronary arteries are not accessible to surface vascular ultrasound; they are assessed by computed tomography angiography or catheter angiography. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with joint bleeding has a normal prothrombin time and an activated partial thromboplastin time of 62 seconds (reference 21 to 35). Which deficiency fits?","choices":["Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor XIII","Fibrinogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time with a normal prothrombin time points to the intrinsic pathway; factor VIII deficiency is hemophilia A. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A Q wave measures 0.08 seconds, which is two small boxes. How should that be read?","choices":["Normal; a Q wave may last to 0.12 seconds","Too wide; normal is under 0.04 seconds","Normal; a Q wave lasts 0.12 to 0.20 seconds","Too narrow; it should reach 0.12 seconds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal Q wave is under 0.04 seconds (one small box) and low in amplitude, so two small boxes is too wide. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 73-year-old with known heart failure has B-type natriuretic peptide checked at each visit. Which is a recognized use of serial natriuretic peptides?","choices":["Detecting reinfarction after a stent","Diagnosing statin myopathy","Monitoring the course of disease","Screening healthy adults yearly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beyond the rule-out, they monitor the disease course and stratify risk, including cardiovascular mortality and likelihood of readmission. Adjusting treatment to the peptide level has not been shown to improve outcomes. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 70-year-old presents with chest pain and troponin testing is available. Which markers add no diagnostic value for myocardial infarction?","choices":["Troponin I, troponin T and high-sensitivity troponin assays","B-type natriuretic peptide, N-terminal pro-BNP and troponin","Troponin, creatine kinase and C-reactive protein together","Creatine kinase-MB, myoglobin and lactate dehydrogenase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three are listed as no longer used to evaluate cardiac disease. Ordering them for suspected infarction is a classic example of when not to order. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has suspected thrombosis. Which set of three studies makes up the laboratory workup of a thrombotic (clotting) disorder?","choices":["Factor VIII assay, von Willebrand antigen and platelet function analysis","Prothrombin time, partial thromboplastin time and D-dimer","Bleeding time and platelet aggregation","Mean platelet volume alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The thrombotic workup is the prothrombin time, the activated partial thromboplastin time and D-dimer; these differ across thrombus types and most are abnormal in disseminated intravascular coagulation. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a platelet count of 15,000 per microliter (adult reference 140,000 to 400,000). Which finding is expected?","choices":["Bleeding only after major surgery","Petechiae and spontaneous bleeding","Bruising without spontaneous bleeding","Bleeding into joints and muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Below 20,000 per microliter the patient may have spontaneous bleeding and petechiae. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man is told his body is slowly dissolving a clot in a leg vein. Which term names the enzymatic breakdown of fibrin in clots?","choices":["Primary hemostasis","Secondary hemostasis","Fibrinolysis","Thrombus formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fibrinolysis is the enzymatic breakdown of fibrin in clots: tissue plasminogen activator converts plasminogen to plasmin, which degrades the fibrin. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is noted about the length of the right bundle branch?","choices":["It is much longer than the left, despite the left ventricle being the larger chamber","It is much shorter than the left, in proportion to the smaller right ventricle","The two are of equal length","It does not reach the Purkinje network"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the counterintuitiveness is the point: the anatomy does not follow chamber size. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is the blooming artifact, and why does it matter?","choices":["Motion during the scan blurs the coronary arteries","Contrast pools in the ventricle and obscures the valve","Radiation scatter degrades the image at the edges","Heavy calcification appears larger than it is"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The artifact exaggerates the very lesion the study was ordered to assess, which is a limitation worth naming rather than a curiosity. So a stenosis looks more severe. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 62-year-old on lipid-lowering therapy is followed with repeated apoB (apolipoprotein B) levels. Why is apoB suited to that role?","choices":["It is genetic and fixed over life","It is a marker of acute inflammation","It measures HDL particles only","It is modifiable and responds to treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ApoB is a modifiable, treatment-responsive marker used repeatedly to guide and monitor lipid-lowering therapy, unlike lipoprotein(a), which is fixed and measured once. — svCardiac Biomarkers and Lipids.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A region of myocardium shows no tracer uptake on either the stress or the rest images. What does that indicate?","choices":["Inducible ischemia","Normal myocardium","Dead tissue","An artifact of the gamma camera"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uptake requires functional myocardium, so tissue that never lights up is not ischemic but infarcted. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Where is the atrioventricular node located?","choices":["In the upper wall of the right atrium, below the vena cava opening","Across the posterior aspect of the left ventricle, near its wall","At the junction of atria and ventricles, near the coronary sinus","Inside the right ventricle, at the end of the right bundle branch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This position lets it sit between the atrial and ventricular halves of the system, where it adds a short pause in conduction before the ventricles are activated. — Principles of Electrocardiography.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which electrocardiographic changes during an exercise stress test suggest ischemia?","choices":["ST elevation with reciprocal change","ST depression","A prolonged QT interval","Loss of P waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both appear as the heart is worked and are what the continuous monitoring exists to catch. Or the development of premature ventricular contractions. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a fibrinogen of 8.2 grams per liter (reference 2.0 to 4.0). Which risk is increased?","choices":["Hemorrhage after traumatic surgery","Coronary and cerebrovascular disease","Hemophilia","Platelet dysfunction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A fibrinogen above 7.0 grams per liter is a significant risk for both coronary artery and cerebrovascular disease. — Coagulation Studies 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which structures does a transesophageal study show in more detail than a transthoracic one?","choices":["The right ventricle and the tricuspid valve","Left atrium, mitral valve, aorta and coronaries","The pericardium alone","The coronary calcium burden"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the pulmonary artery too. The probe sits directly behind the heart, so the structures that gain are the posterior ones. — Cardiac Imaging and Vascular Studies - Elwaya.pptx, Slide 22"}]};
